{
  "response": "When a response hook returns `None`, `dispatch_hook` does not replace the current response data. In `requests/hooks.py`, `dispatch_hook` stores the hook result in `_hook_data` and updates `hook_data` only when `_hook_data is not None`; therefore, returning `None` leaves the existing `Response` unchanged and dispatch continues to any remaining hooks [Data: Sources (43)].\n\nThe supplied test demonstrates this behavior: the first hook changes `\"Data\"` to `\"ata\"`, the second hook returns `None`, and the third hook then changes the retained value to `\"ta\"`. Thus, `None` means “keep the current response,” not “discard it” [Data: Sources (74)]. The snapshot shows that `requests.sessions` imports and uses `dispatch_hook`, but it does not include the specific `Session.send` call site, so it is insufficient to establish every later step in `Session.send` beyond this hook-dispatch behavior [Data: Sources (52)].",
  "score": 93,
  "follow_up_queries": [
    "Where exactly does Session.send call dispatch_hook, and what object is passed as hook_data?",
    "What happens if a response hook returns a different Response object or raises an exception?"
  ]
}
